<p>A refusal fails that one command, and the model sees its exit status. The broker checks <code>agent.prompt</code> for the sender before the gateway calls a model, denies a capability with no constraint set before Cedar runs, and caches no decision: the next command is authorized afresh.</p>
</section>
<section id="ideas">
<h2>Dekopon rests on four ideas.</h2>
<div class="idea-grid">
<div class="idea" id="credentials">
<h3>The agent never holds a credential.</h3>
<p>A model can name a secret but can’t read one. The broker adds the credential after the provider has built its request, only for a destination inside that credential’s binding, and refuses a response that echoes it. The gateway, model, script and provider never see it.</p>
<p><strong>The endpoint you authorize receives it.</strong> Allowlist a host that reflects headers back in another encoding and the token walks out through the provider’s output. Pick your hosts.</p>
<p><a href="{{ core }}design.md#constitution">Credentials the agent never holds ↗</a> · <a href="{{ core }}security-model.md#endpoint-trust">Endpoint trust ↗</a></p>
</div>
<div class="idea" id="authority">
<h3>Cedar decides who; constraint sets decide how far.</h3>
<p>The gateway attests which chat sender asked; only the broker maps that sender to a principal. Cedar answers allow or deny. Hosts, methods, request and byte ceilings, the timeout and the credential live in constraint sets the owner writes, which no policy reaches: you can edit a policy to broaden who may act, but not to add a host. Each outbound request gets its own Cedar check, which can only narrow.</p>
<p><strong>Rules check declared access, not intent.</strong> A permitted comment can still say something harmful, and the token’s own scope upstream stays the final boundary.</p>
<p><a href="{{ core }}authorization.md">Authorization ↗</a></p>
</div>
<div class="idea" id="memory">
<h3>Older turns replay as summaries without breaking the prompt cache.</h3>
<p>On a route with a journal, the gateway records each turn and writes its summaries in the background. An older turn replays at one rung: the original, a summary from fullest to shortest, or a metadata line. It changes rung only at a repack, when it leaves the hot window or when the model pins it, so consecutive prompts share a byte-identical prefix. A history trimmed from the front, or summarized afresh each turn, would make every request a full-price cache miss. The model can read any retained original back.</p>
<p><strong>Memory is prompt text and never grants authority.</strong> A changed grant drops the history, but the check compares capability names only: tightening a constraint set keeps what the agent read under the looser one.</p>
<p><a href="{{ core }}memory.md#the-ladder">The memory ladder ↗</a></p>
</div>
<div class="idea" id="keyless">
<h3>A coding agent in a VM reaches models without a key.</h3>
<p>A key inside a Firecracker VM is a key whatever runs there can print. So <code>claude</code>, <code>pi</code> or <code>codex</code> calls the gateway’s model proxy through the jail’s egress route. The jail connects over mTLS and names the VM; the proxy maps it to an agent and its models, injects the key and charges the agent’s token budget. The proxy lives in the gateway because the gateway already holds the model keys.</p>
<p><strong>The certificate identifies the jail, not the VM.</strong> A guest that escapes into the jail can name any configured VM and spend that agent’s budget. Budgets count raw tokens with cached input in full and live in gateway memory; at boot the gateway restores them from telemetry, undercounting whatever the exporter dropped.</p>
<p><a href="{{ core }}design.md#keyless-model-access-for-outside-vms">Keyless model access ↗</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="design">
<h2>Two daemons, one script tool, no audit store</h2>
<h3 id="processes">The exposed half never holds provider credentials.</h3>
<p><code>dekopon-gatewayd</code> talks to chat services, runs the model loop and parses whatever the internet sends. <code>dekopon-brokerd</code> holds policy, provider credentials and provider storage. Whoever takes over the gateway gets its chat and model keys and its history, and can speak for every sender its attestor grant covers. Separate UIDs keep provider credentials and policy out of reach. The broker identifies the gateway by kernel peer UID, so there’s no shared secret to leak. Run both under one UID and you keep attribution, not separation. <a href="{{ core }}design.md#why-two-processes-and-two-uids">Why two processes ↗</a></p>
<h3 id="shell">The model writes one script, and its command words are requests.</h3>
<p>One tool schema per capability bloats the prompt and forces many round trips. A bash-like script lets the model write a whole plan in one call, in a language it already writes fluently. A script reaches no filesystem and can't start a host program: <code>gh</code> is a provider word routed to the broker, not the host’s GitHub CLI, and <code>curl</code> is not found unless a provider supplies it. <a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-shell/README.md">dekopon-shell ↗</a></p>
<h3 id="trace">The W3C trace is the audit log.</h3>
<p>Each broker decision and execution is a log record in the message’s trace, beside the prompt and script that caused it, so one trace ID answers “why did this happen” without a second store holding another copy of sensitive data. <strong>Lose or overflow the exporter and those records are gone.</strong> The telemetry store holds every prompt and output; whoever reads it reads every conversation. <a href="{{ core }}design.md#trace-as-audit">Trace as audit ↗</a></p>
</section>
<section id="limits">
<h2>What Dekopon deliberately doesn’t protect</h2>
<ul>
<li>Dekopon doesn’t detect prompt injection or make a model trustworthy; it shrinks what a successful injection can reach. An agent that reads something private and writes somewhere public can be steered to move one to the other: give those capabilities to different agents, or fence the write with a request <code>forbid</code>.</li>
<li>A compromised gateway speaks for every sender inside its attestor grant. Narrow the grant to the workspaces it serves.</li>
<li>If something hostile already runs as root or as the broker’s user, you’ve lost.</li>
<li>Wasmtime runs inside the broker and bounds memory, fuel and time per call. It isn’t claimed as a production sandbox.</li>
<li>Nothing retries or rolls back. A failed call goes back to the model, and a posted comment stays posted.</li>
<li>Shared conversation history replays each participant’s text into prompts the others start.</li>
<li>Cedar governs provider effects, not the gateway’s own chat replies or downloads.</li>
<li>Dekopon isn’t production-hardened and has no multi-tenant transport.</li>
</ul>
